main.content:
==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:been<id:to be>|id=is|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰuH-|*h₁es-|*h₂wes-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|been||to be}}. See below for more.
{{box-top|Further etymology of ''be'' and its conjugated forms}}
The various forms have three separate origins, which were mixed together at various times in the history of English.
* The forms beginning with ''b-'' come from {{inh|en|ang|bēon||to be, become}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*beuną||to be, exist, come to be, become}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*bʰuHyéti|*bʰuH-yé-ti|to grow, become, come into being, appear}}, from the root {{m|ine-pro|*bʰuH-}}. In particular:<!--moved from [[been]]-->
** Now-dialectal use of ''been'' as an infinitive of ''be'' is either from {{inh|en|enm|been|t=to be}} or an extension of the past participle.
** Now-obsolete use of ''been'' as a plural present tense (meaning "are") is from {{inh|en|enm|been}}, {{m|enm|be}} (present plural of {{m|enm|been|t=to be}}, with the ''-n'' leveled in from the past and subjunctive; compare competing forms {{m|enm|aren}}/{{m|enm|are}}).
** Use of ''been'' as a past participle is from {{inh|en|enm|been|}}, {{m|enm|ybeen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ġebēon}}.
* The forms beginning with ''w-'' come from the aforementioned {{inh|en|ang|bēon}}, which shared its past tense with the verb {{m|ang|wesan}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*wesan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wesaną}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₂wes-||to reside}}.
* The remaining forms (''[[am]]'', ''[[are]]'', ''[[is]]'') are also from {{inh|en|ang|wesan||to be}}, {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*wesan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wesaną}}, the present tense of which comes from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₁ésti|*h₁és-ti}}, from the root {{m|ine-pro|*h₁es-}}.
{{box-bottom}}

