====Verb====
{{head|en|verb|highly {{lg|irregular}}—[[#Conjugation|See conjugation table]]}}

# {{ng|As an [[auxiliary verb]]:}}
## {{lb|en|auxiliary}} {{non-gloss|Used with past participles of verbs to form the [[passive voice]].}}
##: {{ux|en|The dog '''was''' saved by the boy.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author={{w|C. K. Ogden}}|title=Psyche: An Annual General and Linguistic Psychology 1920-1952| pageurl=http://books.google.com.br/books?id=43QCzKUXkBYC&pg=PA13#v=onepage&q&f=false| isbn=9780415127790| page=13| publisher=C. K. Ogden| passage=Study courses of Esperanto and Ido have '''been''' broadcast.}}
## {{ng|Used with present participles of verbs to form the [[continuous aspect]].}}
##: {{ux|en|The woman '''is''' walking.}}
##: {{ux|en|I shall '''be''' writing to you soon.}}
##: {{ux|en|We liked to chat while we '''were''' eating.}}
##: {{ux|en|I can't always '''be''' catering to your whimsies.}}
##: {{ux|en|I '''was''' being deadly serious when I made that suggestion.}}
## {{senseid|en|to-infinitive}} {{non-gloss|Used with [[to-infinitive|''to''-infinitives]] of verbs to express intent, obligation, appropriateness{{,}} or relative future occurrence.<!--action, particularly what is due to, intended to, or should happen The fifth example "I am to leave" is indicative, not subjunctive. The sixth is subjunctive, but only incidentally. They are both future periphrastic.-->}}
##: {{ux|en|I '''am''' to leave tomorrow.}}
##: {{ux|en|They '''are''' to stay here until I return.}}
##: {{ux|en|I'd '''been''' [[to've]] been ambassador, but fell seriously ill.}}
##: {{ux|en|How '''were''' they to know the whole exercise was a ruse?}}
##: {{ux|en|They '''were''' to have been married overseas but COVID forced a change of plans.}}
## {{non-gloss|Used with [[past participle]]s of certain [[intransitive verb]]s to form the [[perfect]] [[aspect]].}}
##: {{usex|en|He '''is''' [[finish]]ed.}}
##: {{usex|en|He '''is''' [[go]]ne.}}
##: {{usex|en|He '''is''' [[come]].|q=archaic}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|I|iv|page=133|passage=''They '''are''' not yet come back.''|footer=instead of the modern ''They '''have''' not yet come back.''}}
##* {{RQ:KJV|Revelation|18|2|text=And he cryed mightily with a strõg voyce, saying, Babylon the great '''is''' fallen, '''is''' fallen, and '''is''' become the habitation of deuils{{nb...}}}}
##* Matthew 28:6 (various translations, from the King James Version of '''1611''' to Revised Version of '''1881'''):
##*: He is not here; for he '''is''' risen{{nb...}}.
##* '''1922''', {{w|A. E. Housman}}, {{w|Last Poems}} XXV, l.13, [https://archive.org/details/lastpoems00hous/page/51/mode/1up page 51]:
##* {{RQ:McCarthy Blood Meridian|chapter=I|page=4|passage=His origins '''are''' become remote as is his destiny and not again in all the world’s turning will there be terrains so wild and barbarous to try whether the stuff of creation may be shaped to man’s will or whether his own heart is not another kind of clay.}}
## {{lb|en|AAVE|Caribbean|Ireland|auxiliary|not conjugated}} To tend to do, often do; {{non-gloss|marks the [[w:Habitual be|habitual aspect]].}}
#: {{ux|en|Yo, you see? I told ya. I don't '''be''' lying.}}
# {{n-g|As a [[copulative]] [[verb]]:}}
## {{senseid|en|to exist somewhere}}{{lb|en|with {{m|en|there}}, or dialectally {{m|en|it}}, as {{glossary|dummy pronoun|dummy subject}}}} To [[exist]].
##: {{ux|en|There '''is''' just one woman in town who can help us.}}
##: {{ux|en|{{lb|en|or, dialectally:}} It '''is''' just one woman in town who can help us.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|IV|i|page=178|passage=Some men there '''are''' loue not a gaping Pigge: / Some that are mad, if they behold a Cat: / And others, when the bag-pipe sings i’th nose, / Cannot containe their Vrine for affection.}}
##* {{RQ:Austen Persuasion|volume=IV|page=170| passage="There '''is''' a sort of domestic enjoyment to be known even in a crowd, and this you had."}}
## {{non-gloss|Used to declare the subject and object identical or equivalent.}}
##: {{ux|en|Hi, I'''’m''' Jim.}}
##: {{ux|en|3 times 5 '''is''' fifteen.}}
##: {{ux|en|These four '''are''' the ones going to the quarter-finals.}}
##: {{ux|en|François Mitterrand '''was''' president of France from 1981 to 1995.}}
##: {{ux|en|This '''is''' how we do it.}}
## {{non-gloss|Used to indicate that a [[predicate]] [[nominal]] applies to the subject.}}
##: {{ux|en|Rex '''is''' a dog.}}
##: {{ux|en|A dog '''is''' an animal.}}
##: {{ux|en|Dogs '''are''' animals.}}
## {{non-gloss|Used to indicate that the subject has the qualities described by an adjective or prepositional phrase.}}
##: {{ux|en|The sky '''is''' blue.}}
##* {{RQ:KJV|Luke|22|42||Saying, Father, if thou '''be''' willing, remooue this cup from me: neuerthelesse, not my will, but thine be done.}}
## {{non-gloss|Used to indicate that the subject has the qualities described by a noun or noun phrase.}}
##: {{ux|en|The sky '''is''' a deep blue today.}}
## {{non-gloss|Used to link a subject to a [[measurement]].}}
##: {{ux|en|This building '''is''' three hundred years old.}}
##: {{ux|en|I '''am''' 75 kilograms.}}
##: {{ux|en|He'''’s''' about 6 feet tall.}}
## {{lb|en|with a cardinal numeral}} {{non-gloss|Used to state the [[age]] of a subject in years.}}
##: {{ux|en|I'''’m''' 20 (years old).}}
## {{lb|en|with a {{glossary|dummy pronoun|dummy subject}} {{m|en|it}}}} {{non-gloss|Used to indicate the time of day.}}
##: {{ux|en|It '''is''' almost eight (o’clock).}}
##: {{ux|en|It'''’s''' 8:30'' [read ''eight-thirty''] ''in Tokyo.}}
##: {{ux|en|What time is it there? It'''’s''' night.}}
## {{lb|en|with {{m|en|since}}}} {{non-gloss|Used to indicate passage of [[time]] since the occurrence of an [[event]].}}
##: {{ux|en|It has '''been''' three years since my grandmother died. (similar to "My grandmother died three years ago", but emphasizes the intervening period)}}
##: {{ux|en|It had '''been''' six days since his departure, when I received a letter from him.}}
## {{lb|en|rare|and|regional|chiefly|in the past tense}} {{n-g|Used to link two noun clauses: a day of the week, recurring date, month, or other specific time (on which the event of the [[main clause]] took place) and a period of time indicating how long ago that day was.}} {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|I saw her Monday '''was''' a week: I saw her a week ago last Monday (a week before last Monday).}}
##: {{ux|en|On the morning of Sunday '''was''' fortnight before Christmas: on the morning of the Sunday that was two weeks before the Sunday prior to Christmas.}}
##* {{RQ:Richardson Clarissa|volume=V|page=97|passage=Miss Lardner (whom you have seen at her cousin Biddulph's) saw you at St James's church on Sunday '''was''' fortnight.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1770|location=Ireland|title=Historical Memoirs of the Irish Rebellion, in the year 1641 {{...}} In a letter to Walter Harris, Esq; [By John Curry.] The fourth edition, with corrections throughout the whole, and large additions, by the author|page=186| passage=And so, without as much as to return home to furnish myself for such a journey, volens, nolens, they prevailed, or rather forced me to come to Dublin to confer with those colonels, and that was the last August '''was''' twelvemonth.}}
##* {{RQ:Scott Guy Mannering|volume=I|chapter=V|pages=79-80|pageref=79|passage=Allow me to recommend some of the kipper—It was John Hay that catched it Saturday '''was''' three weeks.}}
##* {{RQ:Eliot Adam Bede|volume=III|page=53|passage=“Did there come no young woman here—very young and pretty—Friday '''was''' a fortnight, to see Dinah Morris?”}}
##*: John Grey gent delivered out of the Marshalsea about August last by Mr. Secretary and remains in St. Mary Overies.
## To [[pass]] or [[spend]] (time).
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1844|author=Robert Chambers|title=Cyclopedia of English Literature: A History|page=289
|text=and when he compares both sides, he will '''be''' some time at a loss to fix any determination.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1907|author=C. J. Cutcliffe Hyne|title=McTodd|page=232
|text=The bag was crisp with ice, and with my fingerless gloves I '''was''' a bit of time unholing the buttons. But I got the flap turned back at last, and there was Ryan grey-faced and stark.}}
## To take a [[period]] of time.
##: {{usex|en|Dinner will '''be''' ten minutes.}}
##: {{usex|en|It '''was''' an hour before he returned.}}
## {{lb|en|often|impersonal|with {{m|en|it}} as a {{glossary|dummy pronoun|dummy subject}}}} {{non-gloss|Used to indicate ambient conditions such as [[weather]], [[light]], [[noise]] or [[air]] [[quality]].}}
##: {{ux|en|It '''is''' hot in Arizona, but it '''is''' not usually humid.}}
##: {{ux|en|Why '''is''' it so dark in here?}}
## {{lb|en|[[be#English: dynamic conjugation|dynamic / lexical ''be'']]|especially in progressive tenses|conjugated non-suppletively in the present tense|see usage notes}} To exist or behave in a certain way.
##* '''2006''' October 9, Kristin Newman (writer), Barney Stinson (character), ''{{w|How I Met Your Mother}}'', season 2, episode 1:
##: {{ux|en|"What do we do?" "We '''be''' ourselves."}}
##: {{ux|en|Just '''be''' yourself.}}
##: {{ux|en|Why is he '''being''' nice to me?}}
##: {{ux|en|Dad is taking a nap, so we're all '''be'''ing quiet.}}
# {{n-g|As an [[intransitive]] [[lexical verb]]:}}
## {{lb|en|now|usually|literary}} To [[exist]]; to have real existence, to be alive.
##: {{ux|en|The Universe has no explanation: it just '''is'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|That was the week that '''was'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|This is something that was [[meant to be|meant to '''be''']].}}
##* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|Matthew|2|18|iij|Rachel wepynge ffor her chyldren&nbsp;/ and wolde nott be comforted becauſe they '''were''' not.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|III|iii|page=265|passage=To '''be''', or not to '''be''': that is the question: / Whether 'tis nobler in the mind to suffer / The slings and arrows of outrageous fortune, / Or to take arms against a sea of troubles, / And by opposing end them?}}
##* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|chapter=12|book=II|page=351|passage={{...}}it were great sottishnesse, and apparent false-hood, to say, that that '''is''' which is not yet in being, or that already hath ceased from being.}}
##* {{quote-av|en|title=w:Monty Python's Flying Circus| episode=Full Frontal Nudity, {{w|Dead Parrot sketch}}| author={{w|Monty Python}}| date=1969-12-07| role={{w|Mr Praline}}| actor={{w|John Cleese}}| passage=This parrot is no more! It has ceased to '''be'''! It's expired and gone to meet its maker! This is a late parrot! It's a stiff! Bereft of life, it rests in peace! If you hadn't nailed it to the perch it would be pushing up the daisies!}}
##* {{RQ:Time|date=13 December 2004|author=Richard Schickel|title=Not Just an African Story| passage=The genial hotel manager of the past '''is''' no more. Now owner of a trucking concern and living in Belgium, Rusesabagina says the horrors he witnessed in Rwanda "made me a different man."}}
## To remain undisturbed in a certain state or situation.
##: {{ux|en|Let them '''be''' for a few hours.}}
##: {{ux|en|Leave us '''be''' until the guests arrive.}}
## To [[occupy]] a [[place]].
##: {{ux|en|The cup '''is''' on the table.}}
## To [[occur]], to take place.
##: {{ux|en|When will the meeting '''be'''?}}
## {{lb|en|in perfect tenses}} {{non-gloss|[[elliptical|Elliptical]] form of "be here", "go to and return from" or similar, also extending to certain other senses of "go".}}
##: {{ux|en|The postman has '''been''' today, but my tickets have still not yet come.}}
##: {{ux|en|I have '''been''' to Spain many times.}}
##: {{ux|en|We've '''been''' about twenty miles.}}
##: {{ux|en|I have terrible constipation – I haven't '''been''' for several days.}}
##: {{ux|en|I’ve '''been''' into town this morning.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* When ''be'' is used copulatively with a pronoun, traditional grammar puts the pronoun in the [[subjective case]] ({{m|en||I}}, {{m|en||he}}, {{m|en||she}}, {{m|en||we}}, {{m|en||they}}) rather than the [[objective case]] ({{m|en||me}}, {{m|en||him}}, {{m|en||her}}, {{m|en||us}}, {{m|en||them}}), regardless of which side of the copula it is placed. For example, “''I was the masked man''” and “''The masked man was I''” would both be considered correct, while “''The masked man was me''” and “''Me was the masked man''” would both be incorrect. However, most colloquial speech treats the verb ''be'' as transitive, in which case the pronoun is used in the objective case if it occurs after the copula: “''I was the masked man''” but “''The masked man was me''”. This paradigm applies even if the copula is linking two pronouns; thus “''I am her''” and “''She is me''", and “''Am I me?''” (versus the traditional “''I am she''”, “''She is I''”, “''Am I I?''”). However, the use of ''whom'' with a copula is generally considered incorrect and a [[hypercorrection]], though in some cases (especially in sentences involving a {{l|en|''to''-infinitive}} or a perfect tense), such as “''Whom do you want to be?''”, it can come naturally to some speakers; in short, straightforward sentences, such as “''Whom are you?''”, this is much rarer and likelier to be considered incorrect.
* In most copulative and intransitive senses ''[[be]]'' is generally a [[stative verb]] that rarely takes the continuous aspect. See [[:Category:English stative verbs]].

